/* Generated by tools/build-mock-bank.mjs. Do not edit. */
CT.mockBank = [{"id":"basics-0","topic":"basics","q":"Pointer vs reference?","a":"<div><p>A pointer holds an address, can be null, can be re-pointed, and needs <code>*</code> to use. A reference is a permanent alias that must be initialized and cannot be null. Use references for parameters, pointers when \"nothing\" is a valid value or you need to re-point.</p></div>"},{"id":"basics-1","topic":"basics","q":"Stack vs heap?","a":"<div><p>The stack holds locals and is freed automatically when the scope ends; it is fast but small. The heap holds <code>new</code>-allocated objects that live until you free them; it is large but slower and you must manage the lifetime (or let a smart pointer do it).</p></div>"},{"id":"basics-2","topic":"basics","q":"What is a dangling pointer?","a":"<div><p>A pointer to memory that has been freed or has gone out of scope. Dereferencing it is undefined behavior. Setting a pointer to <code>nullptr</code> after <code>delete</code> helps, and smart pointers avoid the problem.</p></div>"},{"id":"basics-3","topic":"basics","q":"<code>const int*</code> vs <code>int* const</code>?","a":"<div><p>Read right to left. <code>const int*</code> is a pointer to a const int (data is read-only, pointer can move). <code>int* const</code> is a const pointer to an int (pointer is fixed, data can change).</p></div>"},{"id":"basics-4","topic":"basics","q":"<code>delete</code> vs <code>delete[]</code>?","a":"<div><p>Memory from <code>new</code> must be freed with <code>delete</code>; memory from <code>new[]</code> must be freed with <code>delete[]</code> so every element's destructor runs. Mixing them is undefined behavior. Deleting <code>nullptr</code> is safe and does nothing.</p></div>"},{"id":"basics-5","topic":"basics","q":"What is undefined behavior?","a":"<div><p>Code the standard places no requirements on: the compiler may assume it never happens, so anything can occur (crash, wrong output, or apparently fine). Examples: out-of-bounds access, null dereference, signed overflow, reading uninitialized memory, data races.</p></div>"},{"id":"basics-6","topic":"basics","q":"How do you pass a large object to a function efficiently?","a":"<div><p>By <code>const</code> reference if the function only reads it, to avoid a copy. By non-const reference if it must modify the caller's object.</p></div>"},{"id":"types-0","topic":"types","q":"When would you use <code>dynamic_cast</code>?","a":"<div><p>For a checked downcast from a polymorphic base to a derived type when you cannot express the need through a virtual function. It returns null (pointers) or throws <code>bad_cast</code> (references) on failure. Frequent use suggests a design problem.</p></div>"},{"id":"types-1","topic":"types","q":"The four named casts?","a":"<div><p><code>static_cast</code> for well-defined conversions, <code>dynamic_cast</code> for checked downcasts, <code>const_cast</code> to add or remove <code>const</code> (modifying a truly const object is UB), <code>reinterpret_cast</code> to reinterpret bits (rarely correct). Never a C cast: it can silently pick any of them.</p></div>"},{"id":"types-2","topic":"types","q":"Difference between <code>const</code> and <code>constexpr</code>?","a":"<div><p><code>const</code> means read-only after initialization (the value may be determined at runtime). <code>constexpr</code> means the value or function can be evaluated at compile time and used in constant expressions.</p></div>"},{"id":"types-3","topic":"types","q":"What does <code>auto</code> deduce, and what are its traps?","a":"<div><p>The type of the initializer with top-level <code>const</code> and references dropped. <code>auto x = {1,2,3}</code> is an <code>initializer_list</code>. <code>auto y = v[0]</code> copies; use <code>const auto&amp;</code>. <code>decltype(expr)</code> keeps the exact type.</p></div>"},{"id":"types-4","topic":"types","q":"What is wrong with <code>if (-1 &lt; 1u)</code>?","a":"<div><p>The signed operand is converted to unsigned, becoming a huge value, so the comparison is false. Avoid mixing signed and unsigned; compile with <code>-Wsign-compare</code>.</p></div>"},{"id":"types-5","topic":"types","q":"Why prefer <code>enum class</code>?","a":"<div><p>Its names are scoped (<code>Level::Error</code>) so they do not pollute the enclosing scope, and it does not convert implicitly to an integer. You can also fix its underlying type.</p></div>"},{"id":"types-6","topic":"types","q":"<code>std::vector&lt;int&gt; v{3, 7}</code> versus <code>v(3, 7)</code>?","a":"<div><p>Braces prefer the <code>initializer_list</code> constructor: two elements, 3 and 7. Parentheses call the (count, value) constructor: three elements, each 7.</p></div>"},{"id":"types-7","topic":"types","q":"What are the three meanings of <code>static</code>?","a":"<div><p>A local variable that lives for the whole program and keeps its value between calls; internal linkage for a global (visible only in this <code>.cpp</code>); a class member shared by all objects.</p></div>"},{"id":"types-8","topic":"types","q":"Which C++17 features do you use?","a":"<div><p><code>std::optional</code>, <code>std::variant</code>, <code>std::string_view</code>, structured bindings, <code>if constexpr</code>, <code>if</code> with initializer, fold expressions and <code>[[nodiscard]]</code>: vocabulary types and clearer code.</p></div>"},{"id":"vocab-0","topic":"vocab","q":"What is a vocabulary type?","a":"<div><p>A small standard type that expresses a common idea in a signature: <code>optional</code> (maybe a value), <code>variant</code> (one of several types), <code>expected</code> (a value or an error), <code>span</code> and <code>string_view</code> (a non-owning view). They replace magic values, tagged unions and pointer-plus-count pairs, so the contract is visible and checked.</p></div>"},{"id":"vocab-1","topic":"vocab","q":"<code>optional</code> versus a pointer for \"may be absent\"?","a":"<div><p><code>optional&lt;T&gt;</code> holds the value itself (no heap allocation, no ownership question) and is empty when absent. A pointer means \"refers to something owned elsewhere\" and can be null. Use <code>optional</code> for a possibly missing <i>value</i>, a pointer for a possibly missing <i>reference</i>.</p></div>"},{"id":"vocab-2","topic":"vocab","q":"What happens on <code>*opt</code>, <code>opt.value()</code> and <code>opt.value_or(x)</code> when it is empty?","a":"<div><p><code>*opt</code> is undefined behaviour, <code>value()</code> throws <code>std::bad_optional_access</code>, and <code>value_or(x)</code> returns <code>x</code>. Test with <code>if (opt)</code> first when you can.</p></div>"},{"id":"vocab-3","topic":"vocab","q":"How does <code>std::variant</code> differ from a <code>union</code>?","a":"<div><p>A variant remembers which alternative is active (<code>index()</code>), destroys the old one and constructs the new one on assignment, works with non-trivial types such as <code>std::string</code>, and throws <code>bad_variant_access</code> if you read the wrong one. A raw union does none of that, and reading the wrong member is undefined behaviour.</p></div>"},{"id":"vocab-4","topic":"vocab","q":"Why is <code>std::visit</code> safer than a chain of <code>if</code> / <code>get_if</code>?","a":"<div><p>The visitor must handle every alternative, so adding a type to the variant makes every incomplete visitor a compile error instead of a silent missing case. It also reads the active alternative without a manual check.</p></div>"},{"id":"vocab-5","topic":"vocab","q":"Variant or virtual functions for a set of message or shape types?","a":"<div><p>A variant when the set of types is closed and you keep adding operations: values are stored inline, there are no allocations or virtual calls, and the compiler checks completeness. Virtual functions when the set of types is open for others to extend and the operations are few.</p></div>"},{"id":"vocab-6","topic":"vocab","q":"<code>optional</code>, <code>expected</code>, exception or error code?","a":"<div><p><code>optional</code> when \"no value\" needs no explanation. <code>expected&lt;T, E&gt;</code> (C++23) when the failure is normal and the reason matters. An exception for rare failures the local code cannot handle, and for constructors. An error code only at C boundaries.</p></div>"},{"id":"vocab-7","topic":"vocab","q":"What does <code>std::span</code> replace, and what is its danger?","a":"<div><p>It replaces a <code>(pointer, count)</code> pair: a non-owning view of contiguous elements that remembers its size and accepts a vector, an array or a part of either, without copying. Like <code>string_view</code> it dangles if the data is destroyed or reallocated while the view is alive.</p></div>"},{"id":"vocab-8","topic":"vocab","q":"How do you return several values from a function?","a":"<div><p>A <code>std::pair</code> or <code>std::tuple</code>, unpacked with structured bindings (<code>auto [a, b] = f();</code>), or a small named struct when the fields need names or the result travels. Not out-parameters, except where you want to reuse a buffer.</p></div>"},{"id":"oop-0","topic":"oop","q":"Why must a base class destructor be virtual?","a":"<div><p>If you <code>delete</code> a derived object through a base pointer and the destructor is not virtual, only the base destructor runs: undefined behavior, and the derived part's resources leak. A virtual destructor makes <code>delete</code> dispatch to the most derived destructor first. Any class meant to be a polymorphic base needs one.</p></div>"},{"id":"oop-1","topic":"oop","q":"What is the difference between overloading and overriding?","a":"<div><p>Overloading: several functions with the same name and different parameters in the same scope, chosen at compile time. Overriding: a derived class provides its own implementation of a base <i>virtual</i> function with the same signature, chosen at runtime.</p></div>"},{"id":"oop-2","topic":"oop","q":"What is a pure virtual function and an abstract class?","a":"<div><p>A pure virtual function is declared <code>= 0</code> and has no required implementation. A class with at least one cannot be instantiated; it defines an interface that derived classes must implement.</p></div>"},{"id":"oop-3","topic":"oop","q":"What is the Rule of Three / Five / Zero?","a":"<div><p>Three: if you define a destructor, copy constructor or copy assignment, define all three. Five: add the move constructor and move assignment. Zero: design classes so none are needed by holding resources in self-managing members like <code>vector</code> and <code>unique_ptr</code>. Remember that declaring a destructor or a copy operation suppresses the move operations, so moves silently become copies.</p></div>"},{"id":"oop-4","topic":"oop","q":"What is object slicing?","a":"<div><p>Copying a derived object into a base-class value discards the derived members and virtual behaviour. Avoid it by passing polymorphic objects by reference or pointer.</p></div>"},{"id":"oop-5","topic":"oop","q":"Can a constructor be virtual? Can a destructor be pure virtual?","a":"<div><p>A constructor cannot be virtual: the vptr is set up by the constructor, so there is no dynamic type yet. A destructor <i>can</i> be pure virtual to make a class abstract, but it still needs a body because derived destructors call it.</p></div>"},{"id":"oop-6","topic":"oop","q":"Composition or inheritance?","a":"<div><p>Prefer composition. Use public inheritance only for a true is-a relationship where you need runtime polymorphism, and make sure it satisfies Liskov substitution.</p></div>"},{"id":"oop-7","topic":"oop","q":"What does <code>explicit</code> do on a constructor and on <code>operator bool</code>?","a":"<div><p>On a constructor it forbids implicit conversions from the argument type (<code>Feet f = 3.0</code> fails). On <code>operator bool</code> it allows the conversion only in contexts such as <code>if</code> and <code>while</code>, not in arithmetic. Mark single-argument constructors <code>explicit</code> by default.</p></div>"},{"id":"oop-8","topic":"oop","q":"What is copy-and-swap?","a":"<div><p>Write the copy constructor and a non-throwing <code>swap</code>, then implement <code>operator=(T other)</code> by value that swaps with the parameter. The copy happens before <code>*this</code> is modified, giving the strong exception guarantee and safe self-assignment with one function.</p></div>"},{"id":"objmodel-0","topic":"objmodel","q":"What is the diamond problem and how is it solved?","a":"<div><p>A class inherits the same base through two paths, so the base's members are duplicated and access is ambiguous. Virtual inheritance (<code>class Left : virtual Base</code>) makes all paths share one base subobject, which the most derived class constructs first. It adds memory and indirection, so prefer interfaces without data or composition.</p></div>"},{"id":"objmodel-1","topic":"objmodel","q":"Why do <code>Reader*</code> and <code>Writer*</code> to the same <code>File</code> hold different addresses?","a":"<div><p>With multiple inheritance the object contains each base subobject at its own offset, each with its own vptr. Converting to the second base adjusts the pointer to that subobject, which is why C casts and <code>reinterpret_cast</code> are wrong here.</p></div>"},{"id":"objmodel-2","topic":"objmodel","q":"What does <code>dynamic_cast</code> need and what does it cost?","a":"<div><p>A polymorphic class (a virtual function) because it reads the type information next to the vtable. It checks the real type at run time: null for pointers, <code>std::bad_cast</code> for references. It is slower than a virtual call, so avoid it on hot paths; repeated use suggests a missing virtual function or a Visitor.</p></div>"},{"id":"objmodel-3","topic":"objmodel","q":"<code>typeid</code> on a polymorphic versus non-polymorphic type?","a":"<div><p>On a polymorphic object <code>typeid(*p)</code> gives the dynamic type. On a type with no virtual function it gives the static type, known at compile time. <code>type_info::name()</code> is implementation-defined and only for diagnostics.</p></div>"},{"id":"objmodel-4","topic":"objmodel","q":"When is private inheritance appropriate?","a":"<div><p>For \"implemented in terms of\": to override a virtual function of the base, access its protected members, or use the empty base optimisation. Otherwise prefer composition. It does not allow conversion to the base outside the class.</p></div>"},{"id":"core-0","topic":"core","q":"What is RAII and why is it important?","a":"<div><p>It binds a resource's lifetime to an object: acquire in the constructor, release in the destructor. Cleanup is automatic and exception-safe because destructors run on every exit path. It replaces <code>try/finally</code> and is why C++ needs no garbage collector.</p></div>"},{"id":"core-1","topic":"core","q":"What is stack unwinding?","a":"<div><p>When an exception propagates, the runtime exits scopes up the call stack, calling the destructors of all fully constructed locals until a handler is found. It is what makes RAII exception-safe.</p></div>"},{"id":"core-2","topic":"core","q":"Why should destructors not throw?","a":"<div><p>Destructors run during unwinding. If one throws while another exception is active, <code>std::terminate</code> is called. Destructors are <code>noexcept</code> by default; catch and handle errors inside them.</p></div>"},{"id":"core-3","topic":"core","q":"What are the exception-safety guarantees?","a":"<div><p>No-throw (never throws: destructors, swap, moves), strong (if it throws, the state is exactly as before: commit or rollback), and basic (nothing leaks and invariants hold, but values may have changed). Get the strong guarantee by doing the work on a copy and publishing with a non-throwing swap.</p></div>"},{"id":"core-4","topic":"core","q":"Why mark move operations <code>noexcept</code>?","a":"<div><p><code>std::vector</code> moves its elements when it grows only if the move cannot throw; otherwise it copies them to keep the strong guarantee. A missing <code>noexcept</code> silently turns every reallocation into a round of copies.</p></div>"},{"id":"core-5","topic":"core","q":"What happens if a <code>noexcept</code> function throws?","a":"<div><p><code>std::terminate</code> is called: the exception does not propagate. So only promise <code>noexcept</code> when it is true.</p></div>"},{"id":"core-6","topic":"core","q":"Exceptions or error codes?","a":"<div><p>Exceptions for rare failures the local code cannot handle (and constructors); return values (<code>optional</code>, error codes, <code>expected</code>) for expected failures and for hot paths where a throw's cost and unpredictability are unacceptable.</p></div>"},{"id":"core-7","topic":"core","q":"How do you run cleanup on every path without <code>finally</code>?","a":"<div><p>Put it in a destructor: a standard RAII wrapper (<code>lock_guard</code>, <code>unique_ptr</code>, <code>fstream</code>) or a small scope guard class. Release the guard on success to commit.</p></div>"},{"id":"moves-0","topic":"moves","q":"What does <code>std::move</code> actually do?","a":"<div><p>Nothing at runtime: it is an unconditional cast to an rvalue reference. It enables the move constructor or move assignment to be chosen. The moved-from object stays valid but unspecified. On a <code>const</code> object it silently copies, because a <code>const</code> rvalue cannot bind to a move constructor.</p></div>"},{"id":"moves-1","topic":"moves","q":"What are lvalues, prvalues and xvalues?","a":"<div><p>An lvalue is a named object with an address. A prvalue is an unnamed temporary (a literal, <code>a + b</code>, a call returning by value). An xvalue is an object marked as movable, such as <code>std::move(x)</code>. Rvalue means prvalue or xvalue. A named rvalue-reference parameter is still an lvalue inside the function.</p></div>"},{"id":"moves-2","topic":"moves","q":"What is copy elision / RVO?","a":"<div><p>The compiler constructs a returned object directly in the caller's storage, skipping the copy or move. In C++17 it is guaranteed when returning a temporary (prvalue). NRVO for a named local is allowed but not required. Do not write <code>return std::move(local)</code>: it prevents it and costs a move.</p></div>"},{"id":"moves-3","topic":"moves","q":"Why mark a move constructor <code>noexcept</code>?","a":"<div><p><code>std::vector</code> moves its elements on reallocation only if the move cannot throw; otherwise it copies them to keep the strong guarantee. Without <code>noexcept</code> every growth becomes a round of copies.</p></div>"},{"id":"moves-4","topic":"moves","q":"What state is a moved-from object in?","a":"<div><p>Valid but unspecified: you may destroy it or assign a new value to it, but not rely on its contents. For standard types it is usually empty.</p></div>"},{"id":"moves-5","topic":"moves","q":"How do you write a class that stores a string argument efficiently?","a":"<div><p>Take the parameter by value and <code>std::move</code> it into the member. An lvalue costs one copy plus one move, an rvalue costs one move, and there is a single function to write.</p></div>"},{"id":"moves-6","topic":"moves","q":"What is the difference between <code>push_back</code> and <code>emplace_back</code>?","a":"<div><p><code>push_back</code> takes an existing object (copied or moved into the container). <code>emplace_back</code> forwards its arguments and constructs the element in place, saving a temporary. Both reallocate the same way.</p></div>"},{"id":"smartptr-0","topic":"smartptr","q":"<code>unique_ptr</code> vs <code>shared_ptr</code> vs <code>weak_ptr</code>?","a":"<div><p><code>unique_ptr</code>: sole owner, move-only, zero overhead. <code>shared_ptr</code>: reference-counted shared ownership with a control block and atomic count. <code>weak_ptr</code>: non-owning observer used to break cycles and to check whether a shared object is still alive (<code>lock()</code>).</p></div>"},{"id":"smartptr-1","topic":"smartptr","q":"Is <code>shared_ptr</code> thread-safe?","a":"<div><p>The reference count is atomic, so copying and destroying <code>shared_ptr</code>s from different threads is safe. The pointed-to object is <b>not</b> protected: concurrent access to it still needs synchronization. Concurrently modifying the same <code>shared_ptr</code> variable is also a race.</p></div>"},{"id":"smartptr-2","topic":"smartptr","q":"Why prefer <code>make_unique</code> / <code>make_shared</code>?","a":"<div><p>No naked <code>new</code>, less typing (and exception safe before C++17), and <code>make_shared</code> allocates the object and its control block once instead of twice.</p></div>"},{"id":"smartptr-3","topic":"smartptr","q":"How do you break a <code>shared_ptr</code> cycle?","a":"<div><p>Make one direction (usually the child's link to its parent) a <code>weak_ptr</code>. It does not count toward ownership, so the objects can be destroyed; use <code>lock()</code> to get a temporary <code>shared_ptr</code> when needed.</p></div>"},{"id":"smartptr-4","topic":"smartptr","q":"How do you pass a smart pointer to a function?","a":"<div><p>If the function only uses the object, pass a reference or raw pointer, not the smart pointer. To transfer ownership, take a <code>unique_ptr</code> by value and have the caller <code>std::move</code>. To share, take a <code>shared_ptr</code> by value. Return <code>unique_ptr</code> from factories.</p></div>"},{"id":"smartptr-5","topic":"smartptr","q":"Why can two <code>shared_ptr</code>s built from the same raw pointer crash?","a":"<div><p>Each creates its own control block and count, so the object is deleted twice. Copy an existing <code>shared_ptr</code>, or inherit <code>enable_shared_from_this</code> when an object must hand out a shared pointer to itself.</p></div>"},{"id":"smartptr-6","topic":"smartptr","q":"Does <code>unique_ptr</code> cost anything compared with a raw pointer?","a":"<div><p>Not with the default deleter: it is the same size and the destructor inlines to a <code>delete</code>. A stateful deleter (a capturing lambda, a function pointer) makes it larger.</p></div>"},{"id":"lambdas-0","topic":"lambdas","q":"What is a lambda and what does the compiler generate?","a":"<div><p>An unnamed function object: the compiler creates a unique class with the captured variables as members and the body as <code>operator()</code>. Because it has its own type, calls can be inlined.</p></div>"},{"id":"lambdas-1","topic":"lambdas","q":"Capture by value vs by reference?","a":"<div><p>By value copies the variable when the lambda is created (const inside unless <code>mutable</code>). By reference stores a reference to the live variable and dangles if the lambda outlives it. Capture by value for lambdas that escape the scope or run on another thread.</p></div>"},{"id":"lambdas-2","topic":"lambdas","q":"How do you move a <code>unique_ptr</code> into a lambda?","a":"<div><p>With a C++14 init-capture: <code>[p = std::move(ptr)] { ... }</code>. A plain capture would need a copy, which <code>unique_ptr</code> forbids.</p></div>"},{"id":"lambdas-3","topic":"lambdas","q":"What does <code>mutable</code> do on a lambda?","a":"<div><p>It makes <code>operator()</code> non-const so the lambda can modify its by-value captures. The changes live in the lambda's own copy, not in the original variable.</p></div>"},{"id":"lambdas-4","topic":"lambdas","q":"<code>std::function</code> vs a template parameter?","a":"<div><p>A template parameter keeps the exact callable type, so the call is inlined and nothing allocates. <code>std::function</code> erases the type: one indirect call, possible allocation, but it can store different callables behind one type.</p></div>"},{"id":"lambdas-5","topic":"lambdas","q":"What is a generic lambda?","a":"<div><p>A lambda with <code>auto</code> parameters (C++14): its <code>operator()</code> is a template, so one lambda works for any argument type.</p></div>"},{"id":"lambdas-6","topic":"lambdas","q":"What is wrong with capturing <code>this</code> by <code>[=]</code>?","a":"<div><p><code>[=]</code> inside a member function captures the <code>this</code> pointer, not a copy of the object, so the lambda dangles if the object is destroyed first. It is deprecated in C++20: write <code>[=, this]</code> or <code>[*this]</code> to copy.</p></div>"},{"id":"strings-0","topic":"strings","q":"When is a <code>string_view</code> dangerous?","a":"When it outlives the string it views: views of temporaries, of locals returned from a function, or of a string that is later modified or reallocated."},{"id":"strings-1","topic":"strings","q":"<code>regex_match</code> versus <code>regex_search</code>?","a":"<code>regex_match</code> must match the whole input. <code>regex_search</code> finds a match anywhere inside it."},{"id":"strings-2","topic":"strings","q":"How do you iterate over every match?","a":"<code>std::sregex_iterator</code> (or <code>std::cregex_iterator</code> for C strings) over the range, compared with a default-constructed end iterator."},{"id":"strings-3","topic":"strings","q":"Which clock for a timeout, and why?","a":"<code>steady_clock</code>, because it is monotonic and unaffected by changes to the system time."},{"id":"strings-4","topic":"strings","q":"Why can <code>seconds = milliseconds</code> not compile but <code>milliseconds = seconds</code> can?","a":"Converting to a coarser unit would truncate, so it requires an explicit <code>duration_cast</code>. Converting to a finer unit is lossless, so it is implicit."},{"id":"strings-5","topic":"strings","q":"What is the small string optimisation?","a":"Short strings (about 15 characters in libstdc++) are stored inside the <code>std::string</code> object, so no heap allocation happens and copying is cheap. Longer strings keep a pointer to a heap block. <code>reserve</code> avoids repeated reallocations when you know the size."},{"id":"strings-6","topic":"strings","q":"<code>from_chars</code> versus <code>stoi</code>?","a":"<code>from_chars</code> (C++17) returns an error code, never throws, never allocates and ignores the locale, so it suits hot paths and feed parsing; check that the end pointer reached the end of the token. <code>stoi</code> throws <code>invalid_argument</code> or <code>out_of_range</code> and is for ordinary code."},{"id":"strings-7","topic":"strings","q":"<code>std::format</code> versus streams versus <code>printf</code>?","a":"<code>std::format</code> (C++20) is type-safe, stateless and readable. Streams are extensible but keep state (<code>std::hex</code> sticks) and are verbose. <code>printf</code> has no type checking, so a wrong specifier is undefined behaviour."},{"id":"strings-8","topic":"strings","q":"Why is <code>while (!f.eof())</code> the wrong way to read a file?","a":"<code>eof</code> becomes true only after a read has failed, so the loop body runs once more with a failed read. Loop on the read itself: <code>while (std::getline(f, line))</code>."},{"id":"strings-9","topic":"strings","q":"Output: what does this print? <code>std::string s = \"abc\"; std::cout &lt;&lt; s.find('z') == std::string::npos;</code>","a":"It does not compile as intended: <code>&lt;&lt;</code> binds tighter than <code>==</code>, so it tries to compare the stream with <code>npos</code>. Parenthesise: <code>std::cout &lt;&lt; (s.find('z') == std::string::npos);</code> prints 1."},{"id":"stl-0","topic":"stl","q":"How do you use your own type as a key in <code>unordered_map</code>, and in <code>map</code>?","a":"<div><p><code>unordered_map</code>: provide <code>operator==</code> and a hash functor (or specialise <code>std::hash</code>) that combines all members; equal objects must hash equally. <code>map</code>: provide a strict weak ordering (<code>operator&lt;</code> or a comparator type). Never <code>&lt;=</code>, and never modify a key while it is stored.</p></div>"},{"id":"stl-1","topic":"stl","q":"Why use <code>try_emplace</code> instead of <code>emplace</code> or <code>insert</code>?","a":"<div><p>If the key exists, <code>try_emplace</code> does nothing and does not touch its arguments, so a moved-in value is not lost. <code>emplace</code> and <code>insert</code> may already have built or moved from the value. It is the right call for \"insert if absent\" with expensive or move-only values.</p></div>"},{"id":"stl-2","topic":"stl","q":"What does <code>std::back_inserter</code> do and when do you need it?","a":"<div><p>It is an output iterator whose assignment calls <code>push_back</code>. Algorithms such as <code>copy</code> and <code>transform</code> write through iterators and do not grow the destination, so with an empty target you would write past the end; <code>back_inserter</code> makes them grow it instead.</p></div>"},{"id":"stl-3","topic":"stl","q":"What are C++20 ranges and views?","a":"<div><p>Ranges algorithms take a whole container (<code>std::ranges::sort(v)</code>) and support projections. Views are lazy, non-owning pipelines (<code>v | views::filter(f) | views::transform(g)</code>) that compute on demand without temporaries. Like <code>string_view</code>, a view dangles if its container dies.</p></div>"},{"id":"stl-4","topic":"stl","q":"How does <code>std::vector</code> grow?","a":"<div><p>When <code>size == capacity</code> it allocates a larger buffer (geometric growth, typically 1.5x to 2x, implementation-defined), moves or copies the elements, and frees the old buffer. This makes <code>push_back</code> amortised O(1), but it invalidates all iterators, pointers and references. <code>reserve</code> avoids the reallocations.</p></div>"},{"id":"stl-5","topic":"stl","q":"<code>map</code> vs <code>unordered_map</code>?","a":"<div><p><code>map</code>: balanced tree, sorted iteration, O(log n), needs <code>operator&lt;</code>, supports range queries. <code>unordered_map</code>: hash table, average O(1) (worst O(n) with many collisions), no order, needs a hash and <code>==</code>. Choose <code>unordered_map</code> for plain lookups and <code>map</code> when you need order.</p></div>"},{"id":"stl-6","topic":"stl","q":"<code>push_back</code> vs <code>emplace_back</code>?","a":"<div><p><code>push_back</code> takes an existing object (a copy or move). <code>emplace_back</code> forwards its arguments to the element's constructor and builds it in place, avoiding a temporary. For simple types there is no difference; for objects with expensive construction <code>emplace_back</code> can save a move.</p></div>"},{"id":"stl-7","topic":"stl","q":"What is the erase-remove idiom?","a":"<div><p><code>std::remove</code>/<code>remove_if</code> only moves the elements to keep to the front and returns the new end; it cannot shrink the container. Pass that to the container's <code>erase</code> to actually delete the tail: <code>v.erase(std::remove_if(...), v.end())</code>. C++20 has <code>std::erase_if</code>.</p></div>"},{"id":"stl-8","topic":"stl","q":"What is a hash collision and how is it handled?","a":"<div><p>Two different keys hash to the same bucket. The standard library's unordered containers resolve it with chaining (a bucket holds a list of entries), so lookups in a heavily colliding bucket degrade toward O(n). A good hash function and a sensible load factor (<code>rehash</code>, <code>reserve</code>) keep it near O(1).</p></div>"},{"id":"stl-9","topic":"stl","q":"Why can't I <code>std::sort</code> a <code>std::list</code>?","a":"<div><p><code>sort</code> needs random-access iterators (it jumps around by index). A list only provides bidirectional iterators, so it has its own member function <code>list::sort</code>.</p></div>"},{"id":"stl-10","topic":"stl","q":"Which container for a task queue? For \"top K\" elements?","a":"<div><p>A queue (<code>std::queue</code>, backed by <code>deque</code>) for FIFO tasks. A <code>priority_queue</code> (heap) for top K: keep a heap of size K and compare against its root: O(n log K).</p></div>"},{"id":"templates-0","topic":"templates","q":"How are template arguments deduced?","a":"<div><p>From the arguments of the call, by the shape of the parameter. By value (<code>T x</code>): <code>const</code> and references are dropped and arrays decay to pointers. By reference (<code>T&amp; x</code>): <code>const</code> and the array size are kept. <code>T&amp;&amp;</code> is a forwarding reference. No conversions take part, so <code>maxOf(3, 2.5)</code> fails; name <code>T</code> explicitly.</p></div>"},{"id":"templates-1","topic":"templates","q":"What are alias templates and variable templates?","a":"<div><p>An alias template is a parameterised <code>using</code> (<code>template&lt;class T&gt; using Vec = std::vector&lt;T&gt;;</code>), which a <code>typedef</code> cannot be. A variable template is a constant or variable per type (<code>template&lt;class T&gt; constexpr T zero = T{};</code>); <code>std::is_integral_v</code> is one.</p></div>"},{"id":"templates-2","topic":"templates","q":"What happens when you use a function template?","a":"<div><p>The compiler deduces the template arguments from the call, then <b>instantiates</b> a concrete function for that combination of types (once per distinct combination across the program), and compiles it like normal code. The template itself generates no code.</p></div>"},{"id":"templates-3","topic":"templates","q":"What is a forwarding reference?","a":"<div><p>A <code>T&amp;&amp;</code> parameter where <code>T</code> is deduced for that very function template (or <code>auto&amp;&amp;</code>). It binds to lvalues and rvalues alike: for an lvalue <code>T</code> becomes <code>X&amp;</code> and reference collapsing makes the parameter <code>X&amp;</code>; for an rvalue <code>T</code> is <code>X</code> and the parameter is <code>X&amp;&amp;</code>. <code>std::forward</code> then preserves that category.</p></div>"},{"id":"templates-4","topic":"templates","q":"What is perfect forwarding, and why do we need it?","a":"<div><p>Passing arguments through a wrapper to another function without changing their value category or constness, so the callee can still choose copy vs move correctly. Without it you would need an overload per combination of lvalue/rvalue/const for every parameter.</p></div>"},{"id":"templates-5","topic":"templates","q":"Explain SFINAE.","a":"<div><p>If substituting deduced types into a function template's signature yields an invalid type or expression, that candidate is removed from the overload set rather than raising an error. <code>std::enable_if</code> exploits it to enable a function only for suitable types. C++17 <code>if constexpr</code> and C++20 concepts are the clearer modern tools.</p></div>"},{"id":"templates-6","topic":"templates","q":"Full vs partial specialization?","a":"<div><p>Full: all template arguments fixed. Partial: a pattern with some parameters still free (<code>Foo&lt;T*&gt;</code>), allowed for class templates and variable templates but not for function templates (use overloading).</p></div>"},{"id":"templates-7","topic":"templates","q":"Why must template definitions be in headers?","a":"<div><p>The compiler needs the body to instantiate it in each translation unit that uses a particular type. If only the declaration is visible, no instantiation happens and the linker cannot find the function. Alternative: explicit instantiation in one <code>.cpp</code>.</p></div>"},{"id":"templates-8","topic":"templates","q":"What is a variadic template and how do you process the pack?","a":"<div><p>A template taking zero or more arguments via a parameter pack (<code>typename... Args</code>). Expand with <code>args...</code>. Process by recursion with a base case, or in C++17 with a fold expression like <code>(args + ...)</code>.</p></div>"},{"id":"templates-9","topic":"templates","q":"Templates vs inheritance for polymorphism?","a":"<div><p>Templates give static (compile-time) polymorphism: no runtime cost and inlinable, but the type must be known when compiling and each type duplicates code. Inheritance with virtual functions gives dynamic (runtime) polymorphism: one code path and heterogeneous collections, at the cost of an indirection and heap-allocated objects.</p></div>"},{"id":"compiletime-0","topic":"compiletime","q":"What does SFINAE stand for and why does it exist?","a":"Substitution Failure Is Not An Error: an invalid substitution while deducing a template removes that candidate from overload resolution instead of causing a hard error. It is what makes <code>enable_if</code> overload selection work."},{"id":"compiletime-1","topic":"compiletime","q":"<code>std::is_same_v&lt;int, const int&gt;</code>?","a":"False. They are different types. <code>const</code> and references are part of the type, which is why traits like <code>std::decay_t</code> and <code>std::remove_cvref_t</code> exist."},{"id":"compiletime-2","topic":"compiletime","q":"<code>constexpr</code> function versus <code>consteval</code> function?","a":"<code>constexpr</code> may run at compile time or run time. <code>consteval</code> must run at compile time and is a compile error otherwise."},{"id":"compiletime-3","topic":"compiletime","q":"Why is <code>if constexpr</code> better than <code>if</code> in a template?","a":"The discarded branch is not instantiated, so code that is invalid for some <code>T</code> does not break the build for that <code>T</code>."},{"id":"compiletime-4","topic":"compiletime","q":"How do you avoid writing the factorial struct by hand today?","a":"Write <code>constexpr int factorial(int n)</code> and use it in a <code>constexpr</code> context. It is shorter, readable and can also run at run time."},{"id":"patterns-0","topic":"patterns","q":"How do you make a Singleton thread-safe in C++?","a":"<div><p>Use a function-local static (Meyers Singleton). Since C++11 its initialization is guaranteed thread-safe and happens exactly once. Alternatives are <code>std::call_once</code> with an <code>std::once_flag</code>, or creating it before threads start. Hand-written double-checked locking is easy to get wrong. Remember the object's own methods still need locking if they mutate shared state.</p></div>"},{"id":"patterns-1","topic":"patterns","q":"What is wrong with Singleton?","a":"<div><p>It is global mutable state with a nice name: hidden dependencies, tight coupling, hard to test or mock, awkward lifetime and destruction order, and a problem for multithreading. Prefer passing a reference (dependency injection) unless a single shared instance is truly inherent.</p></div>"},{"id":"patterns-2","topic":"patterns","q":"Strategy vs State?","a":"<div><p>Both delegate behaviour to a swappable object. Strategy: the client selects an interchangeable algorithm and strategies are independent. State: the context changes its behaviour as its own state changes, and transitions are driven by the states or context.</p></div>"},{"id":"patterns-3","topic":"patterns","q":"What problems can Observer cause?","a":"<div><p>Dangling observers (destroyed but still registered), modification of the observer list during notification, unexpected notification order, cascading updates and performance issues, and thread-safety issues. Fixes: RAII detach, <code>weak_ptr</code>, iterate over a copy, and do not hold locks while calling out.</p></div>"},{"id":"patterns-4","topic":"patterns","q":"Why return <code>unique_ptr</code> from a factory?","a":"<div><p>It expresses that the caller takes sole ownership, prevents leaks, and the caller can convert it to a <code>shared_ptr</code> when sharing is needed. Returning a raw owning pointer is ambiguous and error-prone.</p></div>"},{"id":"patterns-5","topic":"patterns","q":"How do you replace a virtual-function Strategy in modern C++?","a":"<div><p>With a callable: store a <code>std::function</code> (runtime flexibility) or take a template parameter (zero overhead, compile-time choice). A lambda becomes the strategy, and no class hierarchy is needed.</p></div>"},{"id":"patterns-6","topic":"patterns","q":"What is the Open/Closed principle?","a":"<div><p>Software entities should be open to extension but closed to modification: you add new behaviour (a new strategy, a new shape type registered in a factory) without editing the existing, tested code.</p></div>"},{"id":"patterns2-0","topic":"patterns2","q":"What is the Command pattern and how does it give you undo?","a":"<div><p>It turns a request into an object with <code>execute</code> (and <code>undo</code>). The invoker keeps executed commands on a stack; undo pops the last one and calls its <code>undo</code>. Because commands are objects they can also be queued, logged, sent and replayed (orders, thread-pool tasks, macros).</p></div>"},{"id":"patterns2-1","topic":"patterns2","q":"Decorator versus inheritance?","a":"<div><p>A decorator wraps an object with the same interface and adds behaviour, chosen and stacked at run time. Inheritance fixes the combination at compile time and multiplies classes for every combination. Streams with buffering or compression, and logging wrappers, are decorators.</p></div>"},{"id":"patterns2-2","topic":"patterns2","q":"Adapter versus Decorator versus Facade?","a":"<div><p>An adapter changes an interface to the one a client expects. A decorator keeps the interface and adds behaviour. A facade offers a simpler interface to a whole subsystem. <code>std::stack</code> and <code>std::queue</code> are adapters.</p></div>"},{"id":"patterns2-3","topic":"patterns2","q":"When would you use a Builder?","a":"<div><p>When an object has many optional or related parts, so a constructor would take a long argument list, or when it must be validated before use. Chain named setters and validate in <code>build()</code>. For simple aggregates, designated initializers (C++20) may be enough.</p></div>"},{"id":"patterns2-4","topic":"patterns2","q":"Visitor with virtual functions or with <code>std::variant</code>?","a":"<div><p>Virtual functions make adding a type easy and adding an operation hard. A <code>variant</code> with <code>std::visit</code> makes adding an operation easy (one new visitor), keeps values inline without allocation or virtual calls, and the compiler flags every visitor that misses a new alternative. Use it when the set of types is closed, such as message types.</p></div>"},{"id":"patterns2-5","topic":"patterns2","q":"How would you model a FIX or exchange session?","a":"<div><p>As a state machine: states such as Disconnected, LoggedOn and Stopped, each holding only its own data (for example the next expected sequence number), and an <code>onEvent</code> function that returns the next state. A <code>variant</code> of state structs with <code>std::visit</code> makes invalid combinations unrepresentable and avoids virtual calls.</p></div>"},{"id":"threads-0","topic":"threads","q":"What is the difference between a process and a thread?","a":"<div><p>A process has its own address space; threads of one process share memory but each has its own stack and registers. Thread creation and switching are cheaper, communication is easy (shared memory) but needs synchronization.</p></div>"},{"id":"threads-1","topic":"threads","q":"What is a data race? Is it the same as a race condition?","a":"<div><p>A data race is unsynchronised concurrent access to the same memory with at least one write: undefined behavior in C++. A race condition is the wider class of bugs where correctness depends on timing, which can exist even without a data race (two correctly locked steps that needed to be one atomic operation).</p></div>"},{"id":"threads-2","topic":"threads","q":"What happens if a <code>std::thread</code> is destroyed while still joinable, and why?","a":"<div><p><code>std::terminate</code> is called. The alternatives are worse: an implicit <code>join</code> can hide long waits, and an implicit <code>detach</code> leaves a running thread using locals that may be gone (undefined behavior). So make the thread unjoinable on every path: join in an RAII guard, or use <code>std::jthread</code> (C++20), which joins automatically.</p></div>"},{"id":"threads-3","topic":"threads","q":"<code>lock_guard</code> vs <code>unique_lock</code> vs <code>scoped_lock</code>?","a":"<div><p><code>lock_guard</code>: simplest RAII lock for a scope. <code>unique_lock</code>: flexible (unlock/relock, deferred, movable) and needed by condition variables. <code>scoped_lock</code> (C++17): locks multiple mutexes at once with deadlock avoidance.</p></div>"},{"id":"threads-4","topic":"threads","q":"What is a deadlock and how do you prevent it?","a":"<div><p>Threads wait for each other's locks forever. Needs mutual exclusion, hold-and-wait, no preemption and circular wait. Prevent with a consistent global lock order, <code>std::scoped_lock</code>/<code>std::lock</code> for multiple mutexes, short critical sections, no calls to foreign code under a lock, or <code>try_lock</code>.</p></div>"},{"id":"threads-5","topic":"threads","q":"Why does <code>condition_variable::wait</code> need a predicate / a loop?","a":"<div><p>Spurious wakeups (a thread may wake without a notification) and because the state may change again between the notification and the thread reacquiring the lock. The predicate re-checks the real condition.</p></div>"},{"id":"threads-6","topic":"threads","q":"Why must the shared condition be modified under the mutex?","a":"<div><p>Otherwise the waiter could check the condition (false), then the notifier changes it and notifies, then the waiter goes to sleep: a lost wakeup. The mutex makes check-and-sleep atomic with respect to the update.</p></div>"},{"id":"threads-7","topic":"threads","q":"Is a function-local <code>static</code> initialised thread-safely?","a":"<div><p>Yes, since C++11 initialization happens exactly once even with concurrent callers (\"magic statics\"). That is why the Meyers Singleton is thread-safe. Using the object afterwards still needs its own synchronization.</p></div>"},{"id":"threads-8","topic":"threads","q":"My class locks a mutex in every method. Is it thread-safe?","a":"<div><p>Not necessarily. Each call is safe on its own, but a caller's <i>sequence</i> of calls is not atomic. The classic case is a shared stack: <code>if (!s.empty()) { v = s.top(); s.pop(); }</code> races, because another thread can pop between the check and the use. The cause is the interface (it is also true of a lock-free stack), so the fix is to provide whole operations such as <code>std::optional&lt;T&gt; try_pop()</code> that check and remove under one lock.</p></div>"},{"id":"threads-9","topic":"threads","q":"Can threads deadlock without any mutex? What are the practical rules against deadlock?","a":"<div><p>Yes: two threads that <code>join()</code> each other, or any cycle of waits on futures or condition variables. The rules all mean \"do not wait for a thread that might be waiting for you\": avoid nested locks (or take several with <code>scoped_lock</code>), never call user-supplied code or leak references to protected data while holding a lock, lock in a fixed order, and use a lock hierarchy so a wrong order is caught immediately at runtime.</p></div>"},{"id":"threads-10","topic":"threads","q":"How would you find a data race in a large program?","a":"<div><p>Build and run the tests with ThreadSanitizer (<code>-fsanitize=thread</code>), and Helgrind for lock-order problems; review shared state and make sure every access has a lock or is atomic; reduce sharing by design.</p></div>"},{"id":"tasks-0","topic":"tasks","q":"<code>std::async</code> vs <code>std::thread</code>?","a":"<div><p><code>async</code> returns a <code>future</code> carrying the result and any exception, and may use a pool or defer execution (use <code>launch::async</code> to force a thread). <code>thread</code> gives raw control with no result channel. A future from <code>async</code> blocks in its destructor.</p></div>"},{"id":"tasks-1","topic":"tasks","q":"What are <code>promise</code>, <code>future</code> and <code>packaged_task</code>?","a":"<div><p>A <code>promise</code> is the write end of a one-shot channel, a <code>future</code> the read end. A <code>packaged_task</code> wraps a callable and fulfils a future with its result or exception, which is how a pool returns results. A <code>shared_future</code> is a copyable read end for several waiters.</p></div>"},{"id":"tasks-2","topic":"tasks","q":"How would you signal several threads to start at the same moment?","a":"<div><p>A <code>promise&lt;void&gt;</code> whose <code>shared_future</code> every thread waits on, or a <code>std::latch</code>/<code>barrier</code>. The promise is one-shot; for repeated signals use a condition variable.</p></div>"},{"id":"tasks-3","topic":"tasks","q":"Why is the task run outside the lock in a thread-pool worker?","a":"<div><p>Otherwise only one task could run at a time, and the queue would stay locked while a task works. The worker takes the task under the lock, releases it, then runs the task.</p></div>"},{"id":"tasks-4","topic":"tasks","q":"How many threads should a pool have?","a":"<div><p>About <code>hardware_concurrency()</code> for CPU-bound work (more means oversubscription and context switches), more for I/O-bound or blocking work. Use separate pools for mixed workloads.</p></div>"},{"id":"tasks-5","topic":"tasks","q":"What is work stealing?","a":"<div><p>Each worker has its own deque of tasks and pops from it; an idle worker steals from the back of another's deque. Owners and thieves work at opposite ends, so contention is low and load balances automatically. It removes the single shared queue's lock as a bottleneck.</p></div>"},{"id":"tasks-6","topic":"tasks","q":"What is the difference between <code>latch</code> and <code>barrier</code>?","a":"<div><p>A <code>latch</code> is a one-shot countdown: threads count down and others wait until it reaches zero. A <code>barrier</code> is reusable: a fixed group of threads waits for each other at the end of each phase, then all continue together.</p></div>"},{"id":"tasks-7","topic":"tasks","q":"What does a semaphore do that a mutex does not?","a":"<div><p>A counting semaphore allows up to k threads in at once (a permit counter) and can be released by a different thread than the one that acquired it. A mutex is one owner at a time and must be unlocked by its owner.</p></div>"},{"id":"tasks-8","topic":"tasks","q":"How do you stop a thread cleanly?","a":"<div><p>Cooperatively: ask it to stop and let it return. C++20 <code>std::jthread</code> passes a <code>std::stop_token</code> that the loop checks, and its destructor requests the stop and joins. There is no safe forced kill.</p></div>"},{"id":"tasks-9","topic":"tasks","q":"When is a parallel algorithm worth using, and what must you watch?","a":"<div><p>For large inputs and non-trivial work per element. The callback must be thread-safe, <code>reduce</code> needs an associative operation, and small ranges are slower because of thread overhead. Without a parallel backend the call runs serially.</p></div>"},{"id":"atomics-0","topic":"atomics","q":"<code>std::atomic</code> vs mutex?","a":"<div><p>An atomic makes operations on a single variable indivisible, usually lock-free and cheap. A mutex protects any critical section, including invariants over several variables, but may block. Use atomics for counters and flags, mutexes for compound state.</p></div>"},{"id":"atomics-1","topic":"atomics","q":"Is <code>volatile</code> a substitute for <code>atomic</code>?","a":"<div><p>No. <code>volatile</code> prevents some compiler optimisations but gives no atomicity and no inter-thread ordering. Using it for synchronization is a data race.</p></div>"},{"id":"atomics-2","topic":"atomics","q":"What are the memory orders?","a":"<div><p><code>seq_cst</code> (default, strongest, one global order), <code>acquire</code>/<code>release</code> (pair a release store with an acquire load to publish data), <code>acq_rel</code> for read-modify-write, and <code>relaxed</code> (atomicity only). Start with the default; weaken only with a reason and a clear happens-before argument.</p></div>"},{"id":"atomics-3","topic":"atomics","q":"What does release/acquire guarantee?","a":"<div><p>Everything the producer wrote before a release store is visible to a thread that reads the stored value with an acquire load. The pair synchronizes-with, which gives a happens-before edge, so the earlier plain writes are not a data race.</p></div>"},{"id":"atomics-4","topic":"atomics","q":"What does happens-before mean?","a":"<div><p>If A happens-before B, then A's effects are visible to B. Within a thread it is program order; between threads it exists only through synchronization: atomics with matching orders, mutex unlock/lock, thread start and join, futures. Two conflicting accesses without a happens-before relation are a data race.</p></div>"},{"id":"atomics-5","topic":"atomics","q":"When is <code>relaxed</code> enough?","a":"<div><p>When no other data depends on the atomic: a statistics counter, an id generator, a shutdown flag that carries no data. The final value is correct, but it orders nothing else.</p></div>"},{"id":"atomics-6","topic":"atomics","q":"Why can code with a wrong memory order work on x86 and fail on ARM?","a":"<div><p>x86's hardware already orders ordinary loads and stores like release/acquire, so missing orderings are hidden. ARM and POWER reorder far more, so the same code can read stale data there. The C++ model, not the PC you tested on, defines correctness.</p></div>"},{"id":"atomics-7","topic":"atomics","q":"<code>compare_exchange_weak</code> vs <code>strong</code>?","a":"<div><p>Both set a value only if it still equals the expected one. <code>weak</code> may fail spuriously, so it belongs in a retry loop and is cheaper on some CPUs; <code>strong</code> fails only on a real mismatch and suits a single attempt. On failure they load the current value into <code>expected</code>.</p></div>"},{"id":"atomics-8","topic":"atomics","q":"What is the ABA problem?","a":"<div><p>A CAS succeeds because the value is A again, but it went A to B to A in between, and the structure around it changed (for example a node was freed and reused). Fix with a version counter beside the pointer, or hazard pointers or epoch reclamation.</p></div>"},{"id":"atomics-9","topic":"atomics","q":"When is a spinlock appropriate?","a":"<div><p>For very short critical sections on dedicated cores where waiting is shorter than a context switch. It burns CPU while waiting and is dangerous if the holder can be descheduled. Otherwise use a mutex.</p></div>"},{"id":"atomics-10","topic":"atomics","q":"How is a reference count made thread-safe, and with which orders?","a":"<div><p>With an atomic counter: increments can be relaxed, the decrement is <code>acq_rel</code> so the thread that drops it to zero sees all other threads' writes before it destroys the object. <code>shared_ptr</code>'s control block works this way.</p></div>"},{"id":"atomics-11","topic":"atomics","q":"Mutex, fine-grained locks or lock-free: how do you choose?","a":"<div><p>Start with one mutex and measure. Split locks if contention shows up and the structure has independent parts (head and tail of a queue, buckets of a table). Go lock-free only for a proven bottleneck and with a known-correct design, because memory reclamation and ABA make it hard; an SPSC ring buffer is the one case simple enough to write confidently.</p></div>"},{"id":"wire-0","topic":"wire","q":"Why not cast the receive buffer to a struct pointer?","a":"<div><p>Four problems at once: it violates strict aliasing (undefined behavior), the buffer address may not be aligned for the struct, struct padding may not match the wire layout, and the byte order may differ. Check the length, then copy each field out with <code>memcpy</code> or assemble it with shifts.</p></div>"},{"id":"wire-1","topic":"wire","q":"What is struct padding and how do you reduce it?","a":"<div><p>The compiler inserts unused bytes so each member sits at a multiple of its alignment, and pads the end so arrays stay aligned. <code>sizeof</code> can exceed the sum of the members. Order members from largest to smallest alignment; use <code>static_assert(sizeof(...))</code> to guard the size.</p></div>"},{"id":"wire-2","topic":"wire","q":"What does <code>#pragma pack(1)</code> do, and what does it cost?","a":"<div><p>It removes padding so the struct matches a wire layout. The cost: members may be unaligned, which is slower or faults on some CPUs, and references or pointers to them are unsafe. Prefer reading fields with <code>memcpy</code>.</p></div>"},{"id":"wire-3","topic":"wire","q":"What is endianness? Which does the network use?","a":"<div><p>The order of a multi-byte value's bytes in memory. Little-endian stores the least significant byte first (x86, ARM), big-endian the most significant first. Network byte order is big-endian, though a given exchange protocol may differ, so check the spec. Convert at the boundary.</p></div>"},{"id":"wire-4","topic":"wire","q":"How do you read a big-endian 32-bit value portably?","a":"<div><p>Assemble it from the bytes with shifts, widening each byte to <code>uint32_t</code> first: <code>(uint32_t{p[0]} &lt;&lt; 24) | (uint32_t{p[1]} &lt;&lt; 16) | (uint32_t{p[2]} &lt;&lt; 8) | uint32_t{p[3]}</code>. It works on any CPU.</p></div>"},{"id":"wire-5","topic":"wire","q":"What is strict aliasing?","a":"<div><p>The rule that an object may only be accessed through an lvalue of its own type (or a few related types); the compiler assumes this when optimizing. Exceptions: <code>char</code>, <code>unsigned char</code> and <code>std::byte</code> may alias anything. Use <code>memcpy</code> or <code>std::bit_cast</code> to reinterpret bytes.</p></div>"},{"id":"wire-6","topic":"wire","q":"<code>memcpy</code>, <code>bit_cast</code> or a union for type punning?","a":"<div><p><code>memcpy</code> and <code>std::bit_cast</code> (C++20) are well defined and compile to a plain load. Reading a different union member than was written is undefined behavior in C++, even though GCC allows it as an extension.</p></div>"},{"id":"wire-7","topic":"wire","q":"How do you set, clear and test a bit, and check for a power of two?","a":"<div><p>Set <code>x | (1u &lt;&lt; n)</code>; clear <code>x &amp; ~(1u &lt;&lt; n)</code>; test <code>(x &gt;&gt; n) &amp; 1</code>. Power of two: <code>x != 0 &amp;&amp; (x &amp; (x - 1)) == 0</code>. Use unsigned types and keep <code>n</code> below the width.</p></div>"},{"id":"wire-8","topic":"wire","q":"Why should prices not be <code>double</code>?","a":"<div><p>Binary floating point cannot represent most decimals exactly (<code>0.1 + 0.2 != 0.3</code>), so sums and comparisons drift. Store an integer count of ticks (a scaled <code>int64_t</code>): exact, deterministic, and comparable with <code>==</code>. Parse text straight into ticks and watch for overflow when multiplying by quantity.</p></div>"},{"id":"wire-9","topic":"wire","q":"Why <code>std::from_chars</code> in a parser?","a":"<div><p>It converts text to numbers without allocating, throwing or consulting the locale, and reports errors through a return value plus the position where it stopped. <code>stoi</code> throws and needs a <code>std::string</code>; <code>stringstream</code> is slow.</p></div>"},{"id":"wire-10","topic":"wire","q":"How do you detect a gap in sequence numbers, and handle wrap-around?","a":"<div><p>Compare the received number with the expected one: equal is in order, higher is a gap (lost messages: buffer and request a retransmission or snapshot), lower is a duplicate. For wrap-around compute <code>static_cast&lt;int32_t&gt;(received - expected)</code> and look at its sign.</p></div>"},{"id":"latency-0","topic":"latency","q":"Why does tail latency matter more than the average in a feed handler?","a":"<div><p>The rare slow case (p99.9) is what produces a stale book or a missed trade, and the average hides it: a mean of 281 ns can coexist with a p99.9 of 80 microseconds. Measure and report percentiles, and remove sources of unpredictable delay.</p></div>"},{"id":"latency-1","topic":"latency","q":"Why avoid <code>new</code> on the hot path, and what do you do instead?","a":"<div><p>The allocator can take a lock, walk free lists, fragment and call the kernel, so its cost is unpredictable. Allocate at startup: <code>reserve</code> containers, use an object pool or an arena (<code>std::pmr::monotonic_buffer_resource</code>), and use fixed buffers instead of strings and vectors for small data.</p></div>"},{"id":"latency-2","topic":"latency","q":"How does an object pool work?","a":"<div><p>A fixed array of slots, plus a free list threaded through the free slots. Allocation pops the head of the list and constructs the object there with placement new; deallocation destroys it and pushes the slot back. Both are a few instructions with no heap, and when the pool is empty the caller decides what to do.</p></div>"},{"id":"latency-3","topic":"latency","q":"Array of structs versus struct of arrays?","a":"<div><p>AoS keeps one record's fields together, good when you use most fields. SoA keeps each field in its own array, good when a loop uses one or two fields: it streams only the bytes it needs, so each cache line is fully useful. Same results, less memory traffic.</p></div>"},{"id":"latency-4","topic":"latency","q":"What is false sharing and how do you fix it?","a":"<div><p>Two threads writing different variables on the same 64-byte cache line force the line to bounce between cores. Give each variable its own line with <code>alignas(64)</code> or padding, as for the head and tail indices of an SPSC queue.</p></div>"},{"id":"latency-5","topic":"latency","q":"Why is a vector or array usually faster than <code>std::map</code> or <code>std::list</code>?","a":"<div><p>Contiguous memory is cache-friendly and prefetchable; node-based containers chase pointers across the heap and allocate per node. Big-O hides these constants, especially for small and medium sizes.</p></div>"},{"id":"latency-6","topic":"latency","q":"How would you structure an order book?","a":"<div><p>For a bounded price window, an array of quantities indexed by price tick: O(1) updates, no allocation, with the best price tracked and rescanned when its level empties. A hash map from order id to its price and quantity handles cancels. A <code>std::map</code> is simpler but allocates a node per level.</p></div>"},{"id":"latency-7","topic":"latency","q":"Why is an SPSC ring capacity a power of two?","a":"<div><p>The wrap-around becomes <code>counter &amp; (N - 1)</code> instead of a modulo, one cheap instruction, and the head and tail counters can simply keep counting up while <code>tail - head</code> is the size.</p></div>"},{"id":"latency-8","topic":"latency","q":"What do you do when a queue between threads is full?","a":"<div><p>Never let it grow without bound. Choose a policy: block (back-pressure), drop the newest, drop the oldest, or conflate to the latest per instrument, count what was dropped, and recover from the gap if needed. A market-data reader normally cannot block.</p></div>"},{"id":"latency-9","topic":"latency","q":"Why avoid exceptions, virtual calls, locks and logging on the hot path?","a":"<div><p>Each adds cost that is large or unpredictable: a throw costs microseconds, a virtual call blocks inlining and is an indirect branch, a mutex can wait and context-switch, and logging means system calls and formatting. Use error codes, templates or CRTP, single-writer data, and an asynchronous logger fed by a queue.</p></div>"},{"id":"latency-10","topic":"latency","q":"Busy-polling versus blocking, and why pin threads?","a":"<div><p>Blocking saves CPU but a wake-up costs microseconds; busy-polling reacts almost immediately but burns a whole core, so only latency-critical threads spin. Pinning a thread to a core stops migration, which keeps its caches warm and its timing stable.</p></div>"}];
